\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Determine whether the critical dimension $d_*$ for singular free boundaries in the one-phase problem is $5$, $6$, or $7$; describe the singular set in the critical dimension; remove the symmetry assumption from the two-dimensional branch-point regularity result; and find a frequency-function proof that may extend to higher dimensions.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2022/37}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Determine whether the critical dimension $d_*$ for singular free boundaries in the one-phase problem is $5$, $6$, or $7$; describe the singular set in the critical dimension; remove the symmetry assumption from the two-dimensional branch-point regularity result; and find a frequency-function proof that may extend to higher dimensions.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 30005205 (OWR-11101918-004). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
